<h2>The lifecycle of a tainted arbitration</h2>
<p>Two construction contracts for school buildings generated payment disputes. The contractual route — a Dispute Redressal Committee, then arbitration by an appointee of the Chief Engineer under Clause 25 of the General Conditions of Contract — was followed, and awards were rendered in 2023. Those awards did not survive: the appointments were unilateral, made by an interested party&rsquo;s officer, and the awards were set aside on that ground. The contractor returned to the High Court under Section 11(6) for valid appointments so the disputes could be arbitrated afresh.</p>

<h2>The order of Justice Jyoti Singh</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><h3>Clause 25 declared void</h3><p>The clause permitting the Chief Engineer to appoint the arbitrator was held void and unenforceable — a unilateral appointment by an interested party, contrary to the scheme of the 1996 Act as authoritatively interpreted.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Fresh sole arbitrator appointed</h3><p>The Court appointed a sole arbitrator for both references, with the proceedings to be conducted under the aegis of the Delhi International Arbitration Centre.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h3>Concession recorded</h3><p>Counsel for the department accepted the settled legal position — a sign of how completely the law on unilateral appointments has stabilised.</p></div>
